Undo/Redo Overview
=========================

JRebirth provides a complete undo/redo module (`undoredo`) built on top of the Command pattern. It manages parallel stacks of undoable commands, allowing multiple independent undo histories identified by name.


Quick Start
=========================

Follow these steps to add undo/redo to an existing JRebirth application:

1. **Add the Maven dependency** for the `undoredo` module (it transitively pulls in `core`):

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.jrebirth.af</groupId>
    <artifactId>undoredo</artifactId>
    <version>${jrebirth.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

2. **Create an undoable command** by extending `DefaultUndoableCommand` and implementing `init`, `undo`, and `redo`.
3. **Wire action buttons** to your command, passing a stack name as `WaveData` (see `UndoRedoWaves.STACK_NAME` below).
4. **Wire undo/redo buttons** to `UndoCommand` and `RedoCommand` with the same stack name.

Minimal controller wiring (from the showcase):

```java
// Shared stack name for all commands on this editor
static final WaveData<String> stackName =
        WBuilder.waveData(UndoRedoWaves.STACK_NAME, "main");

// Undo / redo buttons
linkCommand(view().getUndoButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, UndoCommand.class, stackName);
linkCommand(view().getRedoButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, RedoCommand.class, stackName);

// Action that registers itself on the stack automatically
linkCommand(view().getAddButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, MyUndoableCommand.class, stackName);
```

When the user triggers `MyUndoableCommand`, `AbstractUndoableCommand` captures state in `init()`, registers the command on the named stack, and calls `redo()`. Undo and redo buttons pop from the stacks managed by `UndoRedoService`.


The system revolves around these key elements:

* **Undoable** — Marker interface for commands that support undo/redo.
* **AbstractUndoableCommand** — Base class that implements the undo/redo lifecycle.
* **UndoRedoService** — Maintains the command stack and the undone stack.
* **StackUpCommand** — Registers a new undoable command on a named stack and executes its `redo()`.
* **UndoCommand / RedoCommand** — Pop commands from respective stacks and invoke `undo()` or `redo()`.


Undoable Interface
=========================

`Undoable` is the marker interface that any command must implement (directly or via `AbstractUndoableCommand`) to participate in the undo/redo system. It extends `Command`, so any undoable command is also a regular JRebirth command.


AbstractUndoableCommand
=========================

This is the base class for all undoable commands. It handles the undo/redo lifecycle automatically:

1. **First execution**: calls `init(Wave)` to capture state, then delegates to `StackUpCommand` which calls `redo()`.
2. **Subsequent calls with UNDO_REDO flag**: dispatches to either `undo()` or `redo()` based on the boolean flag.


```java
/**
 * The Class AbstractUndoableCommand is the base class for all other undoable command.
 *
 * @param <WB> The WaveBean type used for this command (by default you can use the WaveBean interface)
 *
 * @author Sébastien Bordes
 */
public abstract class AbstractUndoableCommand<WB extends WaveBean> extends AbstractSingleCommand<WB> implements Undoable<WB> {

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void perform(final Wave wave) throws CommandException {

        // Get the undo/redo flag to know which action to perform
        if (wave.contains(UndoRedoWaves.UNDO_REDO)) {

            // The flag is true perform undo action
            if (wave.get(UndoRedoWaves.UNDO_REDO)) {
                undo();
            } else {
                // The flag is false perform redo action
                redo();
            }

        } else {

            // If the flag is not set we must initialize the command
            init(wave);
            // Get existing wave data
            final List<WaveData<?>> data = wave.waveDatas();
            // and add the undoable command
            data.add(WBuilder.waveData(UndoRedoWaves.UNDOABLE_COMMAND, this));
            // in order to register it into the right command stack
            callCommand(StackUpCommand.class, data.toArray(new WaveDataBase[data.size()]));
        }

    }

    /**
     * Initialize the command internal properties to be able to undo or redo the command.
     *
     * @param wave the wave
     */
    protected abstract void init(final Wave wave);

    /**
     * Undo the command.
     */
    protected abstract void undo();

    /**
     * Do or Redo the command.
     */
    protected abstract void redo();

}
```

Subclasses must implement three abstract methods:

* `init(Wave wave)` — Capture initial state from the wave data (called once on first execution).
* `undo()` — Reverse the action.
* `redo()` — Apply (or re-apply) the action.


UndoRedoWaves
=========================

The `UndoRedoWaves` interface defines the `WaveItem` constants used to carry undo/redo data through waves:


```java
/**
 * The Interface UndoRedoWaves used to define WaveItems used by undo/Redo waves.
 */
public interface UndoRedoWaves {

    /** The name ot the undeo/redo stack to use. */
    WaveItemBase<String> STACK_NAME = new WaveItemBase<String>() {
    };

    /** The undo redo flag to indicate if we must process the {@link AbstractUndoableCommand}.undo method or {@link AbstractUndoableCommand}.redo. */
    WaveItemBase<Boolean> UNDO_REDO = new WaveItemBase<Boolean>() {
    };

    /** The {@link Undoable} command. */
    WaveItemBase<Undoable> UNDOABLE_COMMAND = new WaveItemBase<Undoable>() {
    };

}
```

* `STACK_NAME` — Identifies which named stack to operate on.
* `UNDO_REDO` — Boolean flag: `true` = undo, `false` = redo.
* `UNDOABLE_COMMAND` — The undoable command instance being stacked.


UndoRedoService
=========================

The `UndoRedoService` is the core engine of the undo/redo system. It maintains two synchronized lists per stack instance:

* **commandStack** — Commands that have been executed (available for undo).
* **undoneStack** — Commands that have been undone (available for redo).

Each named stack is a separate `UndoRedoService` instance, keyed by the stack name string.

The service keeps two synchronized lists per stack: executed commands (`commandStack`) and undone commands (`undoneStack`). The three public methods below move commands between stacks and dispatch `undo()` or `redo()` on the affected command:


```java
public class UndoRedoService extends DefaultService {

    /** The command stack. */
    private final List<Undoable> commandStack = Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<Undoable>());

    /** The commands that have been undone. */
    private final List<Undoable> undoneStack = Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<Undoable>());

    /**
     * Stack up a command.
     *
     * Move internal index and execute the command by triggering a Redo Wave Command
     *
     * @param command the command
     */
    public void stackUp(final Undoable command) {

        // Stack up the command
        this.commandStack.add(command);

        // Call the redo method of the Undoable command
        this.commandStack.get(this.commandStack.size() - 1)
                         .run(WBuilder.wave().addDatas(WBuilder.waveData(UndoRedoWaves.UNDO_REDO, false)));
    }

    /**
     * Undo the last command.
     */
    public void undo() {

        // If there is at least one command left to undo
        if (this.commandStack.size() > 0) {
            // Here put the last command on the undone stack
            this.undoneStack.add(this.commandStack.get(this.commandStack.size() - 1));
            // here remove the last command of the command stack
            this.commandStack.remove(this.commandStack.get(this.commandStack.size() - 1));
            // Call Undo method
            this.undoneStack.get(this.undoneStack.size() - 1)
                            .run(WBuilder.wave().addDatas(WBuilder.waveData(UndoRedoWaves.UNDO_REDO, true)));
        } else {
            // begin of stack, do nothing
            LOGGER.info("No more command to undo, begin of stack");
        }
    }

    /**
     * Redo the last command that was undo-ed.
     */
    public void redo() {

        // If there is at least one command to redo
        if (this.undoneStack.size() > 0) {
            // add the last undone command on the command stack
            this.commandStack.add(this.undoneStack.get(this.undoneStack.size() - 1));
            // remove the command to redo of the undone stack.
            this.undoneStack.remove(this.undoneStack.size() - 1);
            // Call Redo method
            this.commandStack.get(this.commandStack.size() - 1)
                             .run(WBuilder.wave().addDatas(WBuilder.waveData(UndoRedoWaves.UNDO_REDO, false)));
        } else {
            // End of stack, do nothing
            LOGGER.info("No more command to redo, end of stack");
        }
    }

}
```

### Stack operations

| Operation | Effect |
|-----------|--------|
| `stackUp(command)` | Adds command to commandStack, calls `redo()` |
| `undo()` | Moves top of commandStack to undoneStack, calls `undo()` |
| `redo()` | Moves top of undoneStack back to commandStack, calls `redo()` |


StackUpCommand
=========================

`StackUpCommand` is the internal command that registers an undoable command on the appropriate named stack. It extracts the stack name and undoable command from the wave, then calls `UndoRedoService.stackUp()`.

This command is called automatically by `AbstractUndoableCommand.perform()` — you rarely need to invoke it directly.


UndoCommand / RedoCommand
=========================

These are the commands you wire to UI buttons:

* **UndoCommand** — Retrieves the `UndoRedoService` for the given stack name and calls `undo()`.
* **RedoCommand** — Retrieves the `UndoRedoService` for the given stack name and calls `redo()`.

Both commands read the stack name from `UndoRedoWaves.STACK_NAME` in the incoming wave.


Usage Example
=========================

### Creating an Undoable Command

Here is a concrete example from the showcase that creates shapes on a canvas with full undo/redo support:


```java
/**
 * The Class CreateShapeCommand is used to create a node and add it to the graphical editor.
 */
@RunInto(RunType.JAT)
public class CreateShapeCommand extends DefaultUndoableCommand<WaveBean> {

    /** The shape type. */
    private ShapeType shapeType;

    /** The created node. */
    private Node createdNode;

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public void init(final Wave wave) {
        this.shapeType = wave.get(UndoAppWaves.SHAPE_TYPE);

    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public void redo() {

        // Build a node according to the shape type
        switch (this.shapeType) {
            case Circle:
                this.createdNode = new Circle();
                ((Circle)this.createdNode).setFill(Color.BLUE);
                this.createdNode.setLayoutY(60);
                this.createdNode.setLayoutY(60);
                ((Circle)this.createdNode).setRadius(30);
                break;
            case Square:
                this.createdNode = new Rectangle();
                ((Rectangle) this.createdNode).setFill(Color.BLUEVIOLET);
                this.createdNode.setLayoutX(50);
                this.createdNode.setLayoutY(100);
                ((Rectangle) this.createdNode).setWidth(30);
                ((Rectangle) this.createdNode).setHeight(30);
                break;
            case Rectangle:
                this.createdNode = new Rectangle();
                ((Rectangle) this.createdNode).setFill(Color.AZURE);
                this.createdNode.setLayoutX(100);
                this.createdNode.setLayoutY(150);
                ((Rectangle) this.createdNode).setWidth(30);
                ((Rectangle) this.createdNode).setHeight(15);
                break;
        }

        // Add to editor
        getModel(UndoModel.class).addShape(this.createdNode);

    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public void undo() {

        // Remove from editor
        getModel(UndoModel.class).removeShape(this.createdNode);

    }

}
```

Key points:

* The `@RunInto(RunType.JAT)` annotation ensures the command runs on the JavaFX Application Thread (since it manipulates nodes).
* `init()` captures the shape type from the wave.
* `redo()` creates the node and adds it to the editor.
* `undo()` removes the node from the editor.

### Wiring Undo/Redo to the UI

The controller uses `linkCommand` to bind buttons to `UndoCommand`, `RedoCommand`, and the custom `CreateShapeCommand`:


```java
public final class UndoController extends DefaultController<UndoModel, UndoView> {

    /** The class logger. */
    private static final Logger LOGGER = LoggerFactory.getLogger(UndoController.class);

    /**
     * Default Constructor.
     *
     * @param view the view to control
     *
     * @throws CoreException if an error occurred while creating event handlers
     */
    public UndoController(final UndoView view) throws CoreException {
        super(view);
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initEventAdapters() throws CoreException {

        // Manage Ui Command Button
        linkCommand(view().getUndoButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, UndoCommand.class, UndoModel.stackName);
        linkCommand(view().getRedoButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, RedoCommand.class, UndoModel.stackName);

        linkCommand(view().getAddCircleButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, CreateShapeCommand.class, WBuilder.waveData(UndoAppWaves.SHAPE_TYPE, ShapeType.Circle), UndoModel.stackName);
        linkCommand(view().getAddSquareButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, CreateShapeCommand.class, WBuilder.waveData(UndoAppWaves.SHAPE_TYPE, ShapeType.Square), UndoModel.stackName);
        linkCommand(view().getAddRectangleButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, CreateShapeCommand.class, WBuilder.waveData(UndoAppWaves.SHAPE_TYPE, ShapeType.Rectangle), UndoModel.stackName);

    }

}
```

Notice how the stack name (`UndoModel.stackName`) is passed as wave data to every command invocation, ensuring all operations target the same named stack.

### Summary Pattern

1. Create a command extending `DefaultUndoableCommand` (or `AbstractUndoableCommand`).
2. Override `init(Wave)`, `undo()`, and `redo()`.
3. Wire action buttons to your custom command (it auto-registers on the stack).
4. Wire undo/redo buttons to `UndoCommand` / `RedoCommand` with the stack name as `WaveData`.


